00:02:38.624 --> 00:02:42.505
Gen: Welcome to Real World
Remediation Pushback.

00:02:42.915 --> 00:02:48.379
This talk is all about what happens
when WCAG meets the real world.

00:02:50.191 --> 00:02:54.513
I'm Gen from Easy A11y Guide, and
in this presentation, we're gonna

00:02:54.519 --> 00:02:56.376
cover four different sections.

00:02:56.960 --> 00:03:02.264
One is some background about different
types of pushback, where they come from.

00:03:02.867 --> 00:03:07.606
Then we're gonna cover the "but
we've done it that way," which is

00:03:07.608 --> 00:03:13.314
we've always done it that way, we've
done it that way before, et cetera.

00:03:13.433 --> 00:03:15.466
Breaking those old habits.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.451
We're gonna get into a specific section
about third-party developers, because

00:03:21.451 --> 00:03:26.844
the reality is we're always using someone
else's code as part of our builds.

00:03:27.256 --> 00:03:30.718
So how do we communicate
with those someone else's?

00:03:32.013 --> 00:03:35.821
And then we're gonna get into
the classic customer confusion.

00:03:36.314 --> 00:03:42.131
Once you start making the remediation
to the website, what happens when

00:03:42.131 --> 00:03:44.582
the customer starts to get confused?

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:50.862
And how can we make things easier for the
customer so that we have fewer complaints?

00:03:53.214 --> 00:03:56.545
Slides and links for this
presentation and others are

00:03:56.545 --> 00:04:01.520
available at easya11yguide.com/learn.

00:04:04.445 --> 00:04:07.911
Some background about remediation
and the different types of

00:04:07.951 --> 00:04:10.876
pushback that we're going to get
from different kinds of people

00:04:13.167 --> 00:04:20.519
WCAG tells you all about what to fix,
how to test something, but it doesn't

00:04:20.529 --> 00:04:28.335
tell you what to do when clients and
third-party customers start saying no.

00:04:30.188 --> 00:04:34.276
I'm gonna talk about different
remediations that I've done using

00:04:34.276 --> 00:04:39.784
my rapid remediation system with
county and local governments, Inc.

00:04:39.788 --> 00:04:46.462
5000 and small local businesses, clients
who are facing lawsuits, and those who

00:04:46.462 --> 00:04:52.483
have essential third-party content that
they can't possibly get rid of, and the

00:04:52.506 --> 00:04:57.471
plugin developers that I've worked with
who are both solo and others who have

00:04:57.591 --> 00:04:59.616
a million installs and a large team.

00:05:04.217 --> 00:05:09.146
At the end of the day, we are always
making websites and interactions for

00:05:09.367 --> 00:05:11.760
people, at least most of the time.

00:05:11.955 --> 00:05:16.559
Now that AI is getting involved, we're
discovering that the same things that

00:05:16.569 --> 00:05:23.716
helped people use something more easily
also help AI use something more easily.

00:05:24.548 --> 00:05:28.857
I know, it's groundbreaking, but people
are starting to wake up and realize

00:05:28.875 --> 00:05:31.563
that accessibility helps everyone.

00:05:33.472 --> 00:05:34.808
People aren't perfect.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:39.055
That means both the visitors
to your website as well as your

00:05:39.056 --> 00:05:41.106
clients and their customers.

00:05:42.421 --> 00:05:46.359
And priorities in the
real world aren't perfect.

00:05:47.266 --> 00:05:52.575
Both people and processes
frequently resist change.

00:05:53.876 --> 00:05:58.800
In this talk, I'm gonna talk to you
about what's happened during the rapid

00:05:58.800 --> 00:06:04.943
remediation process with real stories,
real successes, and real failures.

00:06:08.931 --> 00:06:11.313
The first kind of client is government.

00:06:12.195 --> 00:06:17.166
We're gonna get some specific
pushbacks for government clients.

00:06:18.311 --> 00:06:24.372
One of the ones I run into often is, "We
need to check with legal." The correct

00:06:24.372 --> 00:06:30.835
answer is, "Okay, here's the info that
legal needs." I was working with a county

00:06:30.836 --> 00:06:36.691
government, and when I was going through,
I discovered there were a number of issues

00:06:36.691 --> 00:06:43.709
in their privacy policy, such as missing
contact information, missing dates, and

00:06:43.835 --> 00:06:46.068
some things that were factually incorrect.

00:06:47.274 --> 00:06:52.909
So I needed to itemize out what
legal needed to know to approve

00:06:52.910 --> 00:06:54.513
changes to the privacy policy.

00:06:54.604 --> 00:07:02.333
That meant citing the specific California
rule and also providing them with, "Here's

00:07:02.333 --> 00:07:06.845
what it looks like right now. Here's the
proposed change," so that all they had

00:07:06.866 --> 00:07:09.396
to do was review it and sign off on it.

00:07:09.777 --> 00:07:14.194
This sped things up so much and
actually got it through their system.

00:07:16.478 --> 00:07:18.322
"We need to check with the board."

00:07:19.482 --> 00:07:22.756
Somewhat surprising to me, just
like nonprofits, governments

00:07:22.765 --> 00:07:25.512
sometimes have executive boards
that they have to answer to.

00:07:26.673 --> 00:07:30.642
So one of the things you need
to know is who is going to be

00:07:30.642 --> 00:07:32.337
presenting this to the board.

00:07:32.788 --> 00:07:37.235
You need to make sure that that
person is on board with what they're

00:07:37.235 --> 00:07:40.754
going to be presenting, otherwise
this is gonna be a lost case.

00:07:41.599 --> 00:07:46.647
You also need to ask about the
politics and priorities of that board.

00:07:47.256 --> 00:07:49.498
Are they trying to save
money this quarter?

00:07:50.320 --> 00:07:52.200
Do they have extra budget to use?

00:07:52.864 --> 00:07:55.092
Are they trying to fix
something long-term?

00:07:55.799 --> 00:07:59.669
Who are these people, and what
are their actual priorities?

00:07:59.743 --> 00:08:02.390
Because you need to appeal to them.

00:08:04.515 --> 00:08:06.535
"We must use this third party."

00:08:07.593 --> 00:08:09.298
That's a really common one.

00:08:10.027 --> 00:08:15.795
The government entity above them has
contracted to use this third party.

00:08:15.871 --> 00:08:20.121
They are locked into a three, a
five-year contract with this vendor.

00:08:21.155 --> 00:08:22.451
You can't get out of it.

00:08:23.081 --> 00:08:27.673
So we're gonna have to figure out what
we can do within this existing contract.

00:08:31.232 --> 00:08:37.335
The next kind of customer are businesses,
and the same issues come up with the

00:08:37.342 --> 00:08:40.237
Inc. 5000 and the small business clients.

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:50.567
"We've always built all of our images this
way." That's nice, but this light green

00:08:50.569 --> 00:08:53.861
text on a white background fails people.

00:08:56.146 --> 00:09:00.100
What I've found is that you just
have to show them the failure,

00:09:00.484 --> 00:09:02.961
show them the poor experience.

00:09:03.794 --> 00:09:06.810
It's not real until it's in front of them.

00:09:06.888 --> 00:09:11.334
The website WhoCanUse has a color
checker with a bright sunlight mode.

00:09:12.576 --> 00:09:16.132
The only problem is that's a
small little button, and I have

00:09:16.133 --> 00:09:20.562
an example here of the light green
and white, and it's all washed out

00:09:22.790 --> 00:09:27.478
So I've put together a CSS snippet
so that you can apply that look of

00:09:27.478 --> 00:09:30.817
bright sunlight to an entire website.

00:09:31.349 --> 00:09:36.664
And once you s- you show someone
their wholly washed out website, they

00:09:36.665 --> 00:09:44.557
suddenly go, "Oh, wow, our call to
action isn't even visible." Correct.

00:09:44.891 --> 00:09:46.860
Your call to action isn't even visible.

00:09:48.145 --> 00:09:50.557
You are losing customers because of this.

00:09:53.593 --> 00:09:55.191
The clients who are facing a lawsuit.

00:09:55.951 --> 00:10:01.242
The first time I worked with a client
who was facing a lawsuit, I really

00:10:01.258 --> 00:10:06.657
thought they'd want it done right, but
that's frequently not their priority.

00:10:07.414 --> 00:10:12.649
They were frequently asking for, "Can't we
just do something to make this go away?"

00:10:14.213 --> 00:10:20.909
So I had to explain to them that 45%
of all federal court filed lawsuits are

00:10:20.911 --> 00:10:23.366
against companies that are already sued.

00:10:24.073 --> 00:10:30.069
You are now and forever on a short
list of things that plaintiff

00:10:30.097 --> 00:10:32.005
lawyers are going to look at.

00:10:32.525 --> 00:10:33.854
They pull this history.

00:10:34.227 --> 00:10:35.533
They check your site.

00:10:35.685 --> 00:10:36.872
They sue you again.

00:10:37.457 --> 00:10:41.159
An e-commerce brand that I was
working with was on their third

00:10:41.169 --> 00:10:45.613
lawsuit settlement, and they finally
decided to bring someone outside in.

00:10:47.265 --> 00:10:51.596
They still hadn't hired a developer
who knew how to keyboard test, but we

00:10:51.596 --> 00:10:56.031
did actually make a number of things
on their website more accessible.

00:10:56.790 --> 00:10:59.908
We didn't win everything,
but we definitely got it a

00:10:59.908 --> 00:11:01.966
lot more usable for people.

00:11:03.829 --> 00:11:07.105
And of course, when they're looking
for fast and cheap, I remind them

00:11:07.507 --> 00:11:11.865
that overlays make you 20 to 30
times more likely to be sued.

00:11:12.431 --> 00:11:17.104
Plaintiff attorneys are literally
searching for websites using overlays,

00:11:17.469 --> 00:11:22.183
and that's actually a really easy to
search to do using tools like BuiltWith.

00:11:25.573 --> 00:11:28.188
Essential third-party content providers.

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:32.877
Sometimes there's a contract,
and you can't break it.

00:11:34.382 --> 00:11:39.262
These third parties may be
unwilling or unable to fix content.

00:11:39.771 --> 00:11:44.359
They frequently don't want to make any
breaking changes that could cause a

00:11:44.359 --> 00:11:46.598
whole lot of customer support tickets.

00:11:48.359 --> 00:11:52.431
If it's currently being embedded with
an iframe, then we're going to need

00:11:52.431 --> 00:11:56.738
to turn that into a link that goes off
to their third-party site with some

00:11:56.738 --> 00:12:03.198
warning content above it, warning people
of what the specific issues are, and I

00:12:03.218 --> 00:12:07.817
like to include contact information for
that vendor so that people can start

00:12:07.838 --> 00:12:14.617
emailing them directly because, well,
the squeaky wheel gets the attention

00:12:16.758 --> 00:12:21.309
If it's some sort of essential
functionality, we want to provide at

00:12:21.309 --> 00:12:27.383
least a phone number or an email address
or something else that someone could use.

00:12:27.769 --> 00:12:31.318
Basically, give them a choice
besides just go to a lawyer.

00:12:32.501 --> 00:12:36.678
This happens all the time in
government contracts and pretty

00:12:36.678 --> 00:12:38.311
often in nonprofit ones as well.

00:12:41.311 --> 00:12:42.568
Plugin developers.

00:12:43.459 --> 00:12:46.021
A little knowledge is dangerous.

00:12:46.564 --> 00:12:50.566
There's the Dunning-Kruger rule
where you have a little bit of

00:12:50.566 --> 00:12:54.235
knowledge and you think you know
far more than you actually do.

00:12:54.906 --> 00:12:58.749
For most of these developers,
accessibility is not

00:12:58.752 --> 00:12:59.699
part of their deadline.

00:12:59.913 --> 00:13:04.468
It's not a metric that the developers
on staff are measured against.

00:13:04.919 --> 00:13:09.207
So you need to be really clear on
how to produce the accessibility

00:13:09.218 --> 00:13:11.405
error and why it's a problem.

00:13:12.454 --> 00:13:15.596
Videos go a really long way here.

00:13:17.006 --> 00:13:20.595
Another danger is that they've
read a little about accessibility

00:13:20.875 --> 00:13:25.708
and decide that adding ARIA to
absolutely everything is the answer.

00:13:26.629 --> 00:13:31.078
As we all know, the first rule of
ARIA is none is better than wrong.

00:13:31.742 --> 00:13:34.688
And then there's the
classic pushback on price.

00:13:35.584 --> 00:13:39.946
So I like to remind them of the plumber
story simply because it's relatable.

00:13:41.482 --> 00:13:42.516
Your sink has a problem.

00:13:43.447 --> 00:13:47.357
You spend three hours on YouTube and
Reddit trying to solve this problem.

00:13:48.803 --> 00:13:51.581
The sink still has the exact same problem.

00:13:52.602 --> 00:13:54.425
You finally call the plumber.

00:13:54.901 --> 00:13:57.915
They arrive and they fix
it in under 30 minutes.

00:13:59.997 --> 00:14:04.714
Hard-won knowledge from
hundreds of websites and

00:14:04.909 --> 00:14:08.211
thousands of hours isn't cheap.

00:14:08.939 --> 00:14:12.058
Your plumber isn't, your
accessibility specialist isn't.

00:14:13.064 --> 00:14:15.904
The, "But we've done it that way before-"

00:14:19.248 --> 00:14:25.041
Lots of times you are coming in and
there are a ton of existing processes,

00:14:26.249 --> 00:14:30.199
and I've found the vast majority of
time organizations don't have any

00:14:30.199 --> 00:14:32.746
standard operating procedures or SOPs.

00:14:34.489 --> 00:14:36.161
The good news is there's
nothing to delete.

00:14:37.302 --> 00:14:42.380
The bad news is you get to discover
all of these unwritten habits.

00:14:43.282 --> 00:14:47.784
You get to document the correct way
to do it, and then you get to train

00:14:47.784 --> 00:14:53.309
the people to actually do it this
new way, and to actually reference

00:14:53.312 --> 00:14:58.242
the SOPs when they go to do something
and not just use their old habits.

00:15:00.124 --> 00:15:04.575
You will find that some of your
staff embrace the new things.

00:15:04.613 --> 00:15:08.956
They want to do better, and you'll
find you have some staff who are

00:15:08.957 --> 00:15:11.655
gonna fight you at every single step.

00:15:12.630 --> 00:15:15.977
The key here is leadership
needs to be on board.

00:15:16.686 --> 00:15:18.123
They need to send memos.

00:15:18.265 --> 00:15:20.879
They need to be the ones
to actually enforce this.

00:15:21.052 --> 00:15:22.978
They're the ones who sign the paychecks.

00:15:23.762 --> 00:15:28.299
So you need to find a way to make sure
that you have someone in leadership

00:15:28.510 --> 00:15:31.032
with actual authority in your corner.

00:15:34.014 --> 00:15:35.279
Record videos.

00:15:35.865 --> 00:15:41.592
The most effective way I've found to
teach someone something is with a video,

00:15:42.233 --> 00:15:48.068
and it's really important to go slowly,
speak out loud every single step.

00:15:49.255 --> 00:15:54.235
For example, on the left sidebar,
about halfway down, is job postings.

00:15:54.797 --> 00:15:59.723
With the person icon, click
on job postings, then click

00:15:59.723 --> 00:16:02.038
on add new in the upper left.

00:16:03.034 --> 00:16:07.872
You'll note for accessibility, I both
name the specific thing they're gonna

00:16:07.875 --> 00:16:12.578
be clicking on, and I also tell them,
generally speaking, where it is.

00:16:13.578 --> 00:16:18.558
This is really helpful
because, one, it slows me down.

00:16:19.405 --> 00:16:23.817
People who are trying to follow these
tutorials are gonna be going slowly,

00:16:24.327 --> 00:16:29.508
and if they can push your video window
off to the side and just listen to the

00:16:29.552 --> 00:16:34.254
audio narration of how to get through
everything, that makes it a lot easier

00:16:34.255 --> 00:16:38.072
for them to do rather than starting
and stopping the video 15 times.

00:16:41.956 --> 00:16:42.964
Press releases.

00:16:44.281 --> 00:16:48.688
A lot of different companies and
government entities have press releases.

00:16:49.209 --> 00:16:51.565
This is also true of meeting minutes.

00:16:53.043 --> 00:16:57.531
They have specific formatting guidelines
that they have written in the past.

00:16:58.286 --> 00:17:00.767
These were probably
written for printed paper.

00:17:01.805 --> 00:17:05.725
Sometimes we even have to go through the
legal department to get permission to even

00:17:05.726 --> 00:17:07.802
change them for uploading to the website.

00:17:08.997 --> 00:17:13.605
Why we have to do this is still a bit of
a mystery to me, but we have to do it.

00:17:15.089 --> 00:17:19.108
So we explain to legal again
what it currently looks like,

00:17:19.504 --> 00:17:22.528
the reason why there's a failure,
and the proposed solution.

00:17:23.392 --> 00:17:26.933
That's always the setup that I've
found gets through this the fastest.

00:17:27.999 --> 00:17:31.633
Most of the time, the key changes
are going to be create actual

00:17:31.634 --> 00:17:37.338
headings and update from using the
raw URLs to using actual links.

00:17:40.748 --> 00:17:46.173
The PDFs, newsletters, meeting
minutes that we have over a decade of.

00:17:47.017 --> 00:17:49.594
They're probably in PDF or PNG format.

00:17:51.335 --> 00:17:53.769
So what we're gonna do
is the archive method.

00:17:54.467 --> 00:17:56.626
The old stuff is not getting updated.

00:17:56.930 --> 00:17:58.774
We're not even gonna worry about it.

00:17:59.212 --> 00:18:02.696
We're just gonna mark it as archived
content, say that the following is

00:18:02.697 --> 00:18:04.734
available for archival purposes only.

00:18:04.734 --> 00:18:08.527
If you need an accessible version,
please contact us at this email address.

00:18:11.417 --> 00:18:14.602
And then going forward, we're
going to do things correctly.

00:18:15.636 --> 00:18:19.327
On this one, you need to do
monitoring for at least six

00:18:19.327 --> 00:18:21.994
months, probably more like a year.

00:18:22.731 --> 00:18:26.242
These habits are ingrained, and
they're really hard to break.

00:18:29.752 --> 00:18:31.758
The fix or replace issue.

00:18:32.518 --> 00:18:37.226
I found a lot of times the
correct answer is not to fix

00:18:37.229 --> 00:18:39.685
something, it's to replace it.

00:18:40.541 --> 00:18:48.379
Look at what the client actually
needs and whether or not it needs

00:18:48.379 --> 00:18:50.620
to e- even use this current tool.

00:18:52.168 --> 00:18:54.507
A client had a job postings plugin.

00:18:55.217 --> 00:19:00.358
I took a look at the plugin history, and
the developer didn't usually fix things.

00:19:00.397 --> 00:19:03.418
They didn't respond to a
lot of their help requests.

00:19:04.951 --> 00:19:09.816
This particular plugin even listed
WCAG 2.2 accessibility support,

00:19:09.826 --> 00:19:14.799
semantic HTML, and ARIA attributes,
and it was a flat out lie.

00:19:15.281 --> 00:19:19.280
And I've seen this on quite
a few tools, both from small

00:19:19.282 --> 00:19:21.580
developers and large companies.

00:19:23.749 --> 00:19:26.617
Honestly, at this point, whenever
I see this, I assume it's a lie,

00:19:27.747 --> 00:19:33.712
and it means that the semantic HTML
is missing or wrong, and the ARIA

00:19:33.727 --> 00:19:40.405
attributes are used wrong repeatedly,
and sometimes in very creative ways.

00:19:41.673 --> 00:19:44.757
I didn't even think that you could
assign some ARIA attributes to

00:19:44.757 --> 00:19:49.763
list items, and you can't, but
the developer thought they could.

00:19:54.814 --> 00:19:59.560
Designing and building for the actual
people who are going to be doing the work.

00:19:59.689 --> 00:20:04.564
You have to look at the real skill
set of what the people can do.

00:20:05.150 --> 00:20:10.697
On that job posting issue, what
happened was we threw out the

00:20:10.699 --> 00:20:15.369
plugin and we set up a custom post
type and specific custom fields.

00:20:16.814 --> 00:20:19.633
Nothing had a WYSIWYG editor.

00:20:19.671 --> 00:20:21.487
It was all plain text.

00:20:22.385 --> 00:20:24.173
They just had specific fields.

00:20:24.373 --> 00:20:27.407
They just populated the information
into each of the fields.

00:20:28.133 --> 00:20:31.832
We made a short video going
through exactly how to fill it

00:20:31.860 --> 00:20:38.602
out, where we typed all the example
information in, and now we were set.

00:20:39.429 --> 00:20:45.332
I found that if you try and create a page
template in whatever editor you prefer,

00:20:46.008 --> 00:20:51.415
and then they go to copy it, things
start going off the rails really fast.

00:20:51.556 --> 00:20:57.262
People start moving content, coloring
content, all sorts of interesting things.

00:20:58.474 --> 00:21:02.615
The synced patterns where people
can edit the text but they can't

00:21:02.616 --> 00:21:06.377
actually change the other settings in
the pattern have worked pretty well.

00:21:08.084 --> 00:21:12.061
And at the end of the day, always
assume that everything will be copied

00:21:12.061 --> 00:21:16.550
and pasted out of a Word document,
and that that Word document was

00:21:16.553 --> 00:21:18.615
copy and pasted out of ChatGPT.

00:21:19.161 --> 00:21:24.431
So you're probably going to need to make
sure that things like incorrect ARIA

00:21:24.452 --> 00:21:31.399
attributes for a list have been removed,
as AI does things that are plausible

00:21:31.491 --> 00:21:34.743
but not correct, especially with ARIA.

00:21:38.530 --> 00:21:42.917
You're gonna make a list of
allowed elements and where to

00:21:42.917 --> 00:21:44.949
use those allowed elements.

00:21:45.774 --> 00:21:49.594
With some page builders, like the
built-in block editor, you can

00:21:49.607 --> 00:21:53.688
actually turn off elements that you
don't want people to be able to use.

00:21:55.209 --> 00:21:57.241
With other page builders, you can't.

00:21:58.028 --> 00:22:00.229
So if you can, hide or disable them.

00:22:00.821 --> 00:22:02.841
If you can't, well, write instructions.

00:22:03.744 --> 00:22:07.288
And then make a list of
when to use each element.

00:22:07.784 --> 00:22:13.075
Document everything with screenshots
and videos when possible.

00:22:15.870 --> 00:22:18.140
Then train a verification step.

00:22:18.879 --> 00:22:22.997
When you're publishing content, you
want to train them to follow a short

00:22:23.039 --> 00:22:25.327
checklist that only takes about a minute.

00:22:25.995 --> 00:22:29.777
The reality is, much longer
than that and you'll lose

00:22:29.781 --> 00:22:31.019
people, they won't do the work.

00:22:32.494 --> 00:22:36.599
Then in about two to three
months after they start producing

00:22:36.600 --> 00:22:40.831
accessible content, you're probably
gonna run into the fallback.

00:22:41.336 --> 00:22:44.897
Because things passed,
they stopped testing.

00:22:45.203 --> 00:22:49.261
Because they stopped testing,
they went back to old habits.

00:22:49.961 --> 00:22:55.212
So you'll need to put a calendar reminder
in there to check, because probably

00:22:55.413 --> 00:22:59.324
things will slip about two to three
months after they start going well.

00:23:05.726 --> 00:23:10.025
Your third-party developers and
working with them and their pushback.

00:23:11.405 --> 00:23:18.562
First, they don't speak WCAG, so
don't send them to the WCAG documents

00:23:18.562 --> 00:23:20.084
that are at a college reading level.

00:23:20.637 --> 00:23:22.142
They're not gonna understand it.

00:23:22.282 --> 00:23:23.763
Their eyes will glaze over.

00:23:25.058 --> 00:23:27.365
What they need is nothing fancy.

00:23:28.902 --> 00:23:34.028
You put in the WCAG references
just as support and credibility.

00:23:35.008 --> 00:23:40.759
What they actually need is to know what
the issue is and what success looks like.

00:23:41.827 --> 00:23:47.729
I've found that with AI, I can frequently
use it to figure out what library the

00:23:47.731 --> 00:23:55.490
developer has been using, and then have
it spit out the exact HTML that I need

00:23:55.539 --> 00:24:00.724
using that library, and here's what you,
what you have, here's the reason why

00:24:00.725 --> 00:24:04.983
it's wrong, here's the exact thing you
need to make it look like at the end.

00:24:05.840 --> 00:24:06.555
And you know what?

00:24:06.810 --> 00:24:08.798
That's really, really successful.

00:24:09.590 --> 00:24:12.612
I have an example here
with a dialogue window.

00:24:14.081 --> 00:24:21.086
Starting out, this did-- had a lot of
issues, but we changed it to having

00:24:21.360 --> 00:24:25.863
that it was the role, that it was an
ARIA modal, that we had the ARIA labeled

00:24:25.877 --> 00:24:27.863
by pointing to the correct element.

00:24:28.313 --> 00:24:30.184
We had to fix the heading structure.

00:24:30.220 --> 00:24:31.801
We had to add IDs.

00:24:32.412 --> 00:24:35.642
There were quite a few things that needed
to get changed in the markup, but I

00:24:35.653 --> 00:24:38.047
gave them the exact answer they needed

00:24:40.763 --> 00:24:43.510
This is the rule that I always use.

00:24:45.723 --> 00:24:50.343
So when you send those examples to
your devs, you're gonna make sure

00:24:50.349 --> 00:24:53.218
that you write the accessible answer.

00:24:53.506 --> 00:24:59.271
I use AI for this a lot, but you always
have to proofread and assume that it

00:24:59.272 --> 00:25:01.648
will make something that looks plausible.

00:25:02.220 --> 00:25:05.628
It won't always make something
that's technically correct.

00:25:06.022 --> 00:25:08.082
So always verify for that.

00:25:09.463 --> 00:25:14.560
I've got an example here that I've sent
to a developer before that I added a label

00:25:14.576 --> 00:25:21.569
element and I linked it via the four slash
ID pairing that replaces where they were

00:25:21.569 --> 00:25:28.760
using the ARIA label, which is less rebu-
robust and invisible to sighted users.

00:25:29.183 --> 00:25:34.481
And then I cited the specific to WCAG
criteria one point three point one

00:25:35.045 --> 00:25:38.150
two point four point six about that.

00:25:39.027 --> 00:25:42.094
Then tell them how to actually test this.

00:25:42.553 --> 00:25:44.357
I usually send a video.

00:25:45.561 --> 00:25:48.279
I will record how I'm testing their code.

00:25:48.777 --> 00:25:53.148
If I'm using a screen reader, I will make
sure that the text that screen reader is

00:25:53.148 --> 00:25:55.768
reading out is right there on my screen.

00:25:57.547 --> 00:26:02.924
And then I will identify what's
going on in the DOM, and if I

00:26:02.946 --> 00:26:05.192
can, I will modify it right there.

00:26:05.574 --> 00:26:07.992
I'll pause the video, make
the modifications, and

00:26:07.992 --> 00:26:09.173
then show them what I did.

00:26:09.699 --> 00:26:13.508
If not, I will give them a link
to the correct semantic markup.

00:26:14.236 --> 00:26:18.505
A lot of times with third-party
developers, they can use

00:26:18.507 --> 00:26:20.319
ARIA, but should they?

00:26:20.872 --> 00:26:25.307
The first rule is always that
no ARIA is better than bad ARIA.

00:26:25.721 --> 00:26:31.237
To always use semantic HTML first
and only add ARIA when needed.

00:26:31.751 --> 00:26:37.066
I have seen numerous, numerous
third-party developers who add ARIA

00:26:37.066 --> 00:26:43.584
label and ARIA label behind to almost
everything, and it's usually wrong.

00:26:49.118 --> 00:26:53.579
Here's an example of where we tried
to replace a Gravity form with a

00:26:53.583 --> 00:26:58.292
Zoho form, what happened, and why
the whole process failed at the end.

00:27:00.451 --> 00:27:03.156
This was a 27 email long chain.

00:27:04.511 --> 00:27:09.353
So the first problem was that a number
of the hidden fields which were capturing

00:27:09.369 --> 00:27:12.399
UTMs and click IDs were missing.

00:27:13.787 --> 00:27:14.526
We repaired that.

00:27:15.292 --> 00:27:22.199
Then we had hidden computed fields that
my Gravity form had built, and after a

00:27:22.200 --> 00:27:24.714
bit of back and forth, we got those fixed.

00:27:25.214 --> 00:27:28.288
Then it was phone numbers
needed extensions.

00:27:28.873 --> 00:27:34.761
Well, the form builder within Zoho didn't
support extensions, so we had to do a bit

00:27:34.761 --> 00:27:37.011
of back and forth to get phone numbers in.

00:27:39.249 --> 00:27:44.336
Then we got to a focus state loss
and random jumping around the form.

00:27:46.268 --> 00:27:51.025
We had a whole side chain
for Zoho development to try

00:27:51.025 --> 00:27:52.633
and fix the focus issues.

00:27:54.250 --> 00:28:00.188
Few days later, then we get to focus
state randomly changes mid-form.

00:28:01.291 --> 00:28:06.033
Now, it's okay for your focus state to
be different in certain parts of your

00:28:06.033 --> 00:28:08.525
website, perhaps in a call to action.

00:28:09.773 --> 00:28:13.357
You've got blue background, white
button, and you need to change out

00:28:13.357 --> 00:28:14.844
what your focus state looks like.

00:28:16.451 --> 00:28:20.879
But when it's randomly changing mid-form,
that's really confusing to users.

00:28:22.467 --> 00:28:27.438
And the thing that actually broke this and
caused this entire process to be abandoned

00:28:27.474 --> 00:28:33.254
was Zoho doesn't support autocomplete,
and they don't plan to anytime soon.

00:28:34.261 --> 00:28:39.331
What happened was the address was
randomly ending up in the name field.

00:28:40.692 --> 00:28:44.571
This was really not good for the
customer because they needed the name

00:28:44.573 --> 00:28:49.586
in the name field, and there were a
few other autocomplete issues, and

00:28:49.586 --> 00:28:53.245
this ended up being the complete
showstopper and deal breaker.

00:28:56.502 --> 00:29:01.960
Another thing I often get from third-party
devs is the not in WCAG answer.

00:29:02.931 --> 00:29:06.951
This is usually from the developer
who's trying to do the bare minimum.

00:29:08.530 --> 00:29:13.757
On one site that I was working on,
about half the buttons and links had a

00:29:13.757 --> 00:29:16.098
hover state and the other half didn't.

00:29:17.520 --> 00:29:21.417
I tried to get them to make a
consistent hover state as people

00:29:21.418 --> 00:29:27.672
expect if something is clickable, when
I hover over it, something happens.

00:29:28.442 --> 00:29:30.266
That indicates to me that it's clickable.

00:29:31.562 --> 00:29:35.688
Well, they replied that hover
states aren't a WCAG requirement,

00:29:35.888 --> 00:29:37.922
so they just removed all of them

00:29:40.691 --> 00:29:44.248
I took the win because at least
they removed the hover behavior

00:29:44.259 --> 00:29:46.145
from non-clickable items.

00:29:47.138 --> 00:29:48.978
Sometimes we have to choose our battles.

00:29:51.839 --> 00:29:52.499
Font sizing.

00:29:53.155 --> 00:29:58.947
This has been a recurring nightmare for me
with third-party developers for plugins.

00:30:00.045 --> 00:30:05.109
When they use pixel sizes, they ensure
a consistent experience and have

00:30:05.114 --> 00:30:11.524
fewer support tickets because various
different developers and themes like

00:30:11.555 --> 00:30:15.459
to change the HTML font size on people.

00:30:18.367 --> 00:30:24.411
So the priority for most developers
is to have fewer support tickets, even

00:30:24.413 --> 00:30:27.273
if that means that text resize fails.

00:30:27.330 --> 00:30:32.314
They'd rather fail a WCAG criteria
than have support tickets.

00:30:33.610 --> 00:30:37.309
And since WCAG has no minimum font size

00:30:39.864 --> 00:30:46.991
they say, "Well, so what if it's 10
pixels? There's no font size." For one

00:30:46.996 --> 00:30:53.515
plugin, I ended up having to write over
100 lines of CSS just to fix their fonts.

00:30:54.604 --> 00:30:58.095
So font sizing is just kind
of a recurring issue that you

00:30:58.095 --> 00:30:59.359
just have to keep dealing with.

00:31:02.365 --> 00:31:05.064
Another thing is can
you replace the vendor?

00:31:06.844 --> 00:31:09.070
We were working on charts and graphs.

00:31:09.243 --> 00:31:13.647
The… We sent issues to the
developer, and they handed back a VPAT.

00:31:14.899 --> 00:31:21.120
Now, there's no actual qualifications
required to complete a VPAT, and what

00:31:21.121 --> 00:31:25.272
they handed back was incomplete, it
tested very little of their actual

00:31:25.273 --> 00:31:28.726
functionality, and it was also inaccurate.

00:31:29.603 --> 00:31:34.506
Whoever was completing it either didn't
have skills or was told to make it pass.

00:31:35.461 --> 00:31:36.769
I don't know which was true.

00:31:38.129 --> 00:31:40.041
So we replaced the vendor.

00:31:41.205 --> 00:31:47.178
The payment schedule was on a
monthly payments, and so they just

00:31:47.178 --> 00:31:51.185
canceled the contract and replaced
them with the high charts vendor.

00:31:54.324 --> 00:31:58.917
If you're trying to reach your
third-party developer, then look

00:31:58.920 --> 00:32:00.637
for an accessibility statement.

00:32:01.934 --> 00:32:05.086
If it has it, use the contact info in it.

00:32:05.367 --> 00:32:09.054
If they just send you a
contact page, don't use that.

00:32:09.541 --> 00:32:14.265
Look for the privacy or legal
page and use that email address.

00:32:15.065 --> 00:32:21.036
Yes, accessibility is a legal issue, so
you are using this address in a valid way.

00:32:22.079 --> 00:32:27.394
I always avoid general contact forms as
they frequently go to staff members who

00:32:27.395 --> 00:32:29.905
have absolutely no idea what's going on.

00:32:33.415 --> 00:32:35.986
The final section is customer confusion.

00:32:36.822 --> 00:32:40.480
What happens when you've
remediated something and then

00:32:40.490 --> 00:32:42.172
the customer gets confused by it?

00:32:44.682 --> 00:32:45.771
Date pickers.

00:32:45.900 --> 00:32:49.030
I could probably do an entire
presentation just on date pickers,

00:32:49.495 --> 00:32:50.753
but I'll keep this pretty short.

00:32:52.606 --> 00:32:58.667
Many date pickers are inaccessible,
so what we normally do is replace

00:32:58.668 --> 00:33:02.944
them with text fields that have
masks on them to solve the issue.

00:33:03.727 --> 00:33:08.822
I always make sure to include helper
text that gives the exact format

00:33:08.903 --> 00:33:11.179
that you need to put in your dates.

00:33:12.668 --> 00:33:17.754
Then we get to the question of to
pre-populate or not to pre-populate.

00:33:19.511 --> 00:33:24.828
If there's only going to be one correct
answer, I will normally pre-populate.

00:33:25.650 --> 00:33:33.078
For example, a county website, you were
submitting a issue request and at the

00:33:33.078 --> 00:33:35.498
end of it you sign your name and date.

00:33:36.360 --> 00:33:39.396
The date is always going to
be the date that it's filled

00:33:39.397 --> 00:33:42.476
out, so that is pre-populated.

00:33:43.170 --> 00:33:44.862
People don't have to do anything with it.

00:33:46.298 --> 00:33:54.705
On another website, it was for a furniture
vendor, and this furniture vendor had a

00:33:54.705 --> 00:34:00.605
number of dealers, and the dealers would
submit requests for exact pricing on

00:34:00.610 --> 00:34:03.147
specific configurations of the furniture.

00:34:04.995 --> 00:34:10.714
They like to have about a week of notice
for getting these quotes together.

00:34:12.829 --> 00:34:17.809
For this furniture vendor, they had
dealers and they needed to have quotes for

00:34:17.812 --> 00:34:20.959
specific configurations on the furniture.

00:34:22.675 --> 00:34:25.737
So we put in a default value
that was about a week out.

00:34:27.037 --> 00:34:31.436
The problem was no one ever
changed the default value.

00:34:32.600 --> 00:34:39.039
So whatever was put in as the default,
no one filling out the form ever changed.

00:34:39.123 --> 00:34:44.350
Even though we had language around it to
inform them to change it, they apparently

00:34:44.356 --> 00:34:46.411
all refused to change default values.

00:34:47.318 --> 00:34:52.856
So the lesson here was we had to set
a minimum using JavaScript of at least

00:34:52.866 --> 00:34:59.088
tomorrow's date, so they would get error
messages and they wouldn't be able to

00:34:59.090 --> 00:35:04.239
move on until they actually put in an
ap- appropriate date, because clearly

00:35:04.390 --> 00:35:06.472
no one ever changes the default value.

00:35:07.702 --> 00:35:12.148
For checks on the value, it's always
important to do the checks on blur

00:35:12.189 --> 00:35:14.536
when the element loses its focus.

00:35:15.021 --> 00:35:18.642
You don't want to have someone
partway through filling out a field

00:35:18.705 --> 00:35:20.486
and giving them error messages.

00:35:21.163 --> 00:35:25.337
It really interrupts their process and
can be extremely confusing to people.

00:35:29.115 --> 00:35:31.503
And always test with autofill.

00:35:32.109 --> 00:35:38.085
There's a lot of issues that have happened
with regard to autofill and forms.

00:35:39.377 --> 00:35:42.867
So always make sure that you have
tested those forms with the autofill.

00:35:44.041 --> 00:35:47.417
In the previous example about
Zoho Forms, that's where

00:35:47.437 --> 00:35:48.907
everything went off the rails.

00:35:49.985 --> 00:35:53.819
And whenever you need specific
information, always make sure that

00:35:53.858 --> 00:35:57.055
that information is before the field.

00:35:57.616 --> 00:36:03.382
The reason is autofill providers drop
down the autofill information and

00:36:03.384 --> 00:36:06.441
cover everything below that field.

00:36:06.959 --> 00:36:12.836
So the default information for the
description that many form plugins

00:36:12.848 --> 00:36:17.552
use, putting that information down
there means that that information is

00:36:17.585 --> 00:36:19.888
invisible when the field is selected.

00:36:20.869 --> 00:36:22.300
Not exactly helpful to people.

00:36:27.453 --> 00:36:29.899
The focus versus focus-visible.

00:36:31.198 --> 00:36:33.199
I prefer to use focus.

00:36:33.241 --> 00:36:39.500
It shows the focus state to both
mouse users and to keyboard users.

00:36:40.028 --> 00:36:44.600
For example, if there's a list of links,
someone right-clicks on a link, opens it

00:36:44.600 --> 00:36:49.773
in a new window, explores the link, closes
that window, comes back, the focus shows

00:36:49.783 --> 00:36:51.679
them right where they were in the past.

00:36:54.756 --> 00:36:59.895
Unfortunately, sometimes the appearance
of focus state on a website that hasn't

00:36:59.906 --> 00:37:02.631
had it in the past can confuse users.

00:37:03.266 --> 00:37:08.915
In this case, we use focus-visible to
only show focus state to keyboard users.

00:37:13.208 --> 00:37:15.749
And the item moved.

00:37:16.877 --> 00:37:19.766
If you have visitors who are
visiting a site on a regular

00:37:19.767 --> 00:37:23.258
basis, big banners are essential.

00:37:23.944 --> 00:37:29.915
I have literally put in 36-pixel
font banners on pages because

00:37:30.857 --> 00:37:34.272
that's the only thing that
actually caught people's attention.

00:37:34.877 --> 00:37:39.750
Putting in normal size font text
above it, they will not read it.

00:37:39.814 --> 00:37:40.999
They will not see it.

00:37:41.057 --> 00:37:43.122
They will do their automatic process.

00:37:43.368 --> 00:37:48.520
You have to interrupt them if you are
going to move something in an area where

00:37:48.531 --> 00:37:50.988
people regularly repeat the same process.

00:37:52.553 --> 00:37:58.915
When you're gonna use red to do this, and
I often do, I use a big, bolded dark red.

00:37:59.589 --> 00:38:02.616
Make sure that it's not overly saturated.

00:38:03.404 --> 00:38:09.153
The very saturated reds can be an issue
for a number of people, so don't use them.

00:38:09.412 --> 00:38:11.373
Just knock your saturation down a bit.

00:38:15.614 --> 00:38:20.145
And that is our remediation
pushback in the real world.

00:38:21.058 --> 00:38:25.404
We talked about some background and the
different types of people that we've

00:38:25.446 --> 00:38:31.810
worked with for remediation projects,
how to deal with the "but we've always

00:38:31.811 --> 00:38:37.726
done it that way" answers, how to work
with third-party developers because

00:38:37.753 --> 00:38:41.833
at the end of the day, all of us have
third-party code coming from somewhere,

00:38:42.525 --> 00:38:48.895
and what to do when customers get
confused about the remediation changes.

00:38:51.989 --> 00:38:53.810
I will now open it up to questions.

00:38:54.078 --> 00:38:58.229
If you're looking for where to find me
online, you can do so at my website,

00:38:58.294 --> 00:39:02.154
easya11yguide.com or on LinkedIn.

00:39:02.225 --> 00:39:04.917
I am the only Gen Herres on LinkedIn.

00:39:14.964 --> 00:39:16.208
Chris: Hey, everybody.

00:39:16.732 --> 00:39:18.185
Gen, thank you for being here.

00:39:18.230 --> 00:39:19.940
Amazing presentation.

00:39:20.533 --> 00:39:24.823
We have quite a few questions
to get through, so if you're

00:39:24.823 --> 00:39:25.982
good, let's dive right in.

00:39:26.868 --> 00:39:27.679
Gen: Yeah, let's go ahead.

00:39:28.345 --> 00:39:28.784
Chris: All right.

00:39:28.832 --> 00:39:29.235
Awesome.

00:39:29.261 --> 00:39:33.908
So the first one is, uh, you clearly have
a lot of experience navigating through

00:39:34.259 --> 00:39:37.144
tough accessibility conversations,
barriers to getting accessibility

00:39:37.144 --> 00:39:38.625
recommendations implemented.

00:39:39.089 --> 00:39:44.037
Is there a point at which you decide it's
no longer worth your time or energy and

00:39:44.159 --> 00:39:48.423
allow organizations to experience the
direct consequences of their actions?

00:39:50.373 --> 00:39:50.825
Gen: Yes.

00:39:50.944 --> 00:39:56.172
So, uh, there are always going to be
times where the client says, "Okay,

00:39:56.232 --> 00:40:00.509
I accept that," and I just have them
sign off that they have accepted it.

00:40:01.692 --> 00:40:02.078
I mean-

00:40:02.143 --> 00:40:02.346
Chris: Okay

00:40:02.391 --> 00:40:04.373
…  Gen: you can lead a horse to
water, you can't make him drink.

00:40:05.045 --> 00:40:05.826
But you can have them sign a waiver.

00:40:05.826 --> 00:40:09.216
Chris: So we're putting pen to paper
and we're, we're signing away liability

00:40:09.217 --> 00:40:10.413
at that point with the customer.

00:40:11.201 --> 00:40:11.498
Gen: Mm-hmm.

00:40:11.565 --> 00:40:11.755
Chris: Yeah.

00:40:12.883 --> 00:40:13.169
Okay.

00:40:13.895 --> 00:40:15.176
Uh, next question.

00:40:15.187 --> 00:40:18.357
You recommended using simple
plain text custom fields.

00:40:18.359 --> 00:40:21.889
What are the most common accessibility
issues you see creeping in from content

00:40:21.913 --> 00:40:24.058
editors using the WYSIWYG field?

00:40:25.665 --> 00:40:31.616
Gen: So the WYSIWYG field especially,
the, uh, block editor is much better,

00:40:31.781 --> 00:40:36.841
generally speaking, than the WYSIWYG
because with the WYSIWYG I see all

00:40:36.841 --> 00:40:43.046
the time people copied it out of
ChatGPT, they pasted it into Word or

00:40:43.047 --> 00:40:45.846
Google Docs, they made edits there.

00:40:46.156 --> 00:40:52.344
We somehow got 47 more styling
attributes added in inline CSS, and

00:40:52.344 --> 00:40:57.081
then we copied out of there and we
pasted that into the WYSIWYG editor,

00:40:57.264 --> 00:41:03.540
and that brought over every one of
those 47 different CSS inline issues.

00:41:03.816 --> 00:41:07.872
And so now we have this whole big
blob of text that we have to clean up.

00:41:08.622 --> 00:41:08.998
So I have-

00:41:09.011 --> 00:41:09.107
Chris: Mm-hmm

00:41:09.107 --> 00:41:12.500
… Gen: one client, uh, who does e-commerce.

00:41:12.572 --> 00:41:15.873
So with WooCommerce, the
fields are mostly WYSIWYGs.

00:41:17.082 --> 00:41:19.671
They aren't in the block editor
for, especially when you're

00:41:19.689 --> 00:41:21.190
getting into the custom fields.

00:41:22.138 --> 00:41:26.029
So regularly they messaged me,
"We updated this product and

00:41:26.029 --> 00:41:27.230
now things went pear-shaped."

00:41:28.111 --> 00:41:33.852
And every single time I go into the HTML
side and there's just a whole ton of

00:41:33.883 --> 00:41:40.556
inline CSS, divs, et cetera, and the c-
and we just clean it all out and then

00:41:40.567 --> 00:41:42.556
save it, and magic, it's working again.

00:41:43.736 --> 00:41:44.118
Chris: Mm-hmm.

00:41:44.429 --> 00:41:44.723
Mm-hmm.

00:41:45.837 --> 00:41:46.093
All right.

00:41:46.134 --> 00:41:51.181
Uh, June asks, uh, we've had a situation
where we shared accessibility feedback

00:41:51.183 --> 00:41:54.615
with product vendors, and in one
situation specifically they did it in

00:41:54.616 --> 00:41:58.835
a public venue and were subsequently
threatened to s- like, be sued for

00:41:58.835 --> 00:42:02.404
defamation by that vendor rather
than try to meaningfully address the

00:42:02.404 --> 00:42:04.168
underlying real accessibility issues.

00:42:04.395 --> 00:42:08.002
Have you had situations get ugly like
that, and if so, how did you handle it?

00:42:09.784 --> 00:42:15.462
Gen: I have not had situations get
ugly like that, but I am generally

00:42:15.482 --> 00:42:21.139
pretty careful about who I share
what with and in what venue.

00:42:21.596 --> 00:42:25.996
You'll find that if you look at, like,
a lot of my videos, you'll see that I

00:42:26.612 --> 00:42:30.402
will generally target a state agency.

00:42:30.426 --> 00:42:35.352
So for example, I was using the New
York E-ZPass website to showcase

00:42:35.374 --> 00:42:37.465
accessibility failures for quite a while-

00:42:38.069 --> 00:42:38.090
Chris: Mm-hmm

00:42:38.090 --> 00:42:41.796
… Gen: because those state agencies have
a very clear-cut line that they're

00:42:41.797 --> 00:42:45.926
required to do this, and they are not the
ones who are gonna be coming after you.

00:42:46.408 --> 00:42:49.045
Private companies are the ones
who are gonna come after you.

00:42:50.051 --> 00:42:53.827
So look at the private company, look
at if they have a bank of lawyers.

00:42:54.299 --> 00:42:58.500
If they have a bank of lawyers,
do not make the, uh, feedback

00:42:58.529 --> 00:42:59.926
public without permission.

00:43:02.200 --> 00:43:02.667
Chris: Good advice.

00:43:03.428 --> 00:43:05.758
So another anonymous question here.

00:43:05.759 --> 00:43:08.702
When dealing with government clients
locked into three- to five-year contracts

00:43:08.702 --> 00:43:12.343
with inaccessible third-party vendors,
you recommended providing an alternative

00:43:12.346 --> 00:43:15.996
contract method or contact method, excuse
me, like a phone number or an email.

00:43:16.246 --> 00:43:19.966
How do you handle situations where
the third-party functionality is

00:43:19.968 --> 00:43:24.083
highly complex, such as a multi-step
permit application and a simple

00:43:24.087 --> 00:43:27.038
phone alternative just isn't
scalable for the client support team?

00:43:29.209 --> 00:43:33.984
Gen: So the reality is, if you have
something that can't be used by a

00:43:33.986 --> 00:43:39.112
number of your constituents, you have
to provide them alternative method.

00:43:39.484 --> 00:43:44.008
Like, if they can't fill out a permit
because the permit form's inaccessible,

00:43:44.679 --> 00:43:49.381
you are obligated to provide an
alternative method for them because

00:43:49.417 --> 00:43:50.837
they can't complete the permit.

00:43:51.323 --> 00:43:56.249
And no, asking them to show up in
person is not a reasonable expectation.

00:43:57.042 --> 00:44:01.450
I mean, there was that huge lawsuit
that went for years in California

00:44:01.821 --> 00:44:07.522
about the, the office wouldn't help
a blind woman complete her paperwork,

00:44:08.531 --> 00:44:13.096
and it would have been vastly, vastly
less expensive for them had they

00:44:13.122 --> 00:44:15.326
just helped her fill out the form.

00:44:17.067 --> 00:44:17.283
Chris: Yeah.

00:44:17.452 --> 00:44:19.571
Gen: I mean, they weren't
asking… You know, she wasn't

00:44:19.582 --> 00:44:21.441
asking for anything unreasonable.

00:44:21.441 --> 00:44:24.612
She was just like, "I can't fill out this
form. I can't see it, I can't type it,

00:44:24.632 --> 00:44:31.560
and it, it's not possible." So if you
have these multi-step complex forms, I'm

00:44:31.562 --> 00:44:36.141
sorry, you're going to have to provide
humans to people who can't fill them out.

00:44:37.285 --> 00:44:37.432
Or-

00:44:37.432 --> 00:44:39.298
Chris: And to your point, Gen, that's also

00:44:39.359 --> 00:44:39.796
maybe

00:44:39.797 --> 00:44:39.852
a-

00:44:39.852 --> 00:44:40.333
Gen: It's a five, six, you know-

00:44:40.333 --> 00:44:41.629
… Chris: less expensive process

00:44:42.071 --> 00:44:43.401
…  Gen: a million dollars.

00:44:43.551 --> 00:44:46.942
D- would you rather spend a million
dollars fighting it and losing?

00:44:47.048 --> 00:44:48.145
They still lost.

00:44:48.461 --> 00:44:49.924
They just spent a million
dollars fighting it.

00:44:51.091 --> 00:44:54.460
Or would you rather just deal
with the fact that you're gonna

00:44:54.473 --> 00:44:55.747
have to have people support it?

00:44:56.089 --> 00:44:58.519
It- that is gonna cost a
whole lot less than a million.

00:44:59.432 --> 00:44:59.700
Chris: Mm-hmm.

00:44:59.966 --> 00:45:02.606
Yeah, how many years of a full-time
s- person's salary was that, right?

00:45:03.162 --> 00:45:06.870
Um, all right, so you pointed out
that plugin developers often misuse

00:45:06.897 --> 00:45:10.449
ARIA attributes due to a lack of
accessibility training in their pipeline.

00:45:10.449 --> 00:45:14.077
When you reverse engineer their library
and send them the exact HTML/ARIA

00:45:14.080 --> 00:45:17.303
fixes, what kind of response rate do you
typically see, and how do you proceed

00:45:17.303 --> 00:45:18.877
if they refuse to implement the fix?

00:45:21.035 --> 00:45:26.207
Gen: So generally I start by trying
to kind of feel them out a little bit.

00:45:27.281 --> 00:45:30.867
So for example, several times when
I've, uh, started to work with a

00:45:30.867 --> 00:45:36.183
vendor, we started with just, you
know, "Here's a couple of issues.

00:45:36.242 --> 00:45:40.886
Do you want to fix them?" Unless I even
receive a positive response to, "Do you

00:45:40.886 --> 00:45:44.232
want to fix them?" I, I don't do any more.

00:45:45.042 --> 00:45:45.351
Chris: Mm-hmm.

00:45:45.624 --> 00:45:48.032
Gen: So we've already
got a positive response.

00:45:48.061 --> 00:45:51.434
They have already given a verbal
commitment that they want to fix it.

00:45:51.775 --> 00:45:54.286
Now it's kind of in for
a penny, in for a pound.

00:45:54.659 --> 00:45:57.769
So now you have, you ha- you've
started to get them to say yes.

00:45:57.831 --> 00:46:03.848
It's a lot like working with clients
because you, you get clients to say yes.

00:46:04.082 --> 00:46:08.340
Do you want your website to be
usable by half the population or

00:46:08.340 --> 00:46:09.554
more than half the population?

00:46:10.925 --> 00:46:12.164
More than half?

00:46:12.659 --> 00:46:12.989
Cool.

00:46:13.146 --> 00:46:15.369
Do you want it to work on people's phones?

00:46:16.321 --> 00:46:17.213
Yes.

00:46:17.470 --> 00:46:21.221
So you get them to say yes a few times
to get them to commit to something.

00:46:21.427 --> 00:46:24.352
And so with third-party
developers, I try to get them to

00:46:24.352 --> 00:46:26.662
say yes to commit to something.

00:46:27.776 --> 00:46:28.013
Chris: Mm-hmm.

00:46:28.036 --> 00:46:31.952
Gen: Now that they've already made
some commitments, now they're involved,

00:46:32.619 --> 00:46:37.761
now when I start to send them fixes
and the fix, you know, is not gonna

00:46:37.770 --> 00:46:41.334
take a large amount of their time,
they're gonna keep saying yes.

00:46:42.737 --> 00:46:43.402
Chris: Great advice.

00:46:43.711 --> 00:46:46.921
All right, uh, we got two minutes, so
I'm gonna try to do some rapid fire here.

00:46:46.937 --> 00:46:50.009
You noted that clients looking for
cheap, fast fixes need to understand

00:46:50.024 --> 00:46:53.463
overlays or need to understand that
overlays make them 20 to 30 times

00:46:53.466 --> 00:46:56.574
more likely to face lawsuits, but a
lot of these overlay companies offer

00:46:56.758 --> 00:47:00.272
some form of legal protection, uh,
to their customers that hire them.

00:47:00.288 --> 00:47:03.845
Do you have any specific evidence-based
counterarguments that you use in

00:47:03.846 --> 00:47:06.899
changing their mind when they are
kind of roped in by those promises?

00:47:08.072 --> 00:47:08.533
Gen: Yes.

00:47:08.676 --> 00:47:11.377
Go read the actual terms of service.

00:47:11.612 --> 00:47:13.899
When you read them, you will
see all the limitations.

00:47:14.991 --> 00:47:15.277
Chris: Mm-hmm.

00:47:15.984 --> 00:47:18.736
All right, so the next one
is, do you see VPATs offering

00:47:18.791 --> 00:47:20.309
any form of legal protection?

00:47:20.322 --> 00:47:22.754
So if I use a third-party
vendor, they have a VPAT.

00:47:23.169 --> 00:47:23.460
No?

00:47:24.204 --> 00:47:24.460
Okay.

00:47:25.150 --> 00:47:27.429
That's probably- There you go … quick
enough, so I'll mark that one as done.

00:47:27.647 --> 00:47:31.451
All right, next one, uh, from Daniella:
Do you prioritize certain barriers

00:47:31.453 --> 00:47:34.219
in your audits or just provide
the list of barriers and let the

00:47:34.220 --> 00:47:35.711
client prioritize for themselves?

00:47:36.836 --> 00:47:39.392
Gen: Absolutely always
prioritize for them.

00:47:39.492 --> 00:47:42.458
Start, uh, basically I
have a rapid remediation.

00:47:42.459 --> 00:47:46.122
You can check my YouTube channel
for a bunch of videos on this topic

00:47:46.407 --> 00:47:48.720
where I get into a lot more detail.

00:47:49.297 --> 00:47:54.517
But basically, whatever's going to impact
the most number of people, start at the

00:47:54.517 --> 00:48:00.473
top, start at the repeated elements, and
go grab my YouTube page for more details.

00:48:01.316 --> 00:48:01.624
Chris: Awesome.

00:48:01.907 --> 00:48:02.837
All right, 40 seconds.

00:48:02.880 --> 00:48:06.041
Uh, at what point do you decide to
rebuild a website instead of remediate?

00:48:07.832 --> 00:48:13.496
Gen: It depends on the scope, the
goals, and how bad the current mess is.

00:48:14.618 --> 00:48:14.717
Chris: Mm-hmm.

00:48:15.154 --> 00:48:15.393
Okay.

00:48:15.800 --> 00:48:18.977
Uh, one common issue with WYSIWYG
editors is that it creates duplicate

00:48:19.002 --> 00:48:20.628
IDs, specifically in WordPress.

00:48:20.642 --> 00:48:22.458
Have you come across any such situations?

00:48:23.336 --> 00:48:23.671
Gen: Yes.

00:48:24.564 --> 00:48:24.911
Chris: All right.

00:48:25.309 --> 00:48:28.535
And in 20 seconds, uh, thanks
for the great presentation.

00:48:28.536 --> 00:48:31.385
When you talk pushback, is
there a general area/business

00:48:31.386 --> 00:48:32.752
specialty that is more resistant?

00:48:36.076 --> 00:48:37.041
Gen: Stubborn people.

00:48:37.777 --> 00:48:38.163
Chris: Stubborn people.

00:48:38.163 --> 00:48:38.984
Gen: It, it just comes down to-

00:48:38.984 --> 00:48:39.636
Chris: So irrespective-

00:48:39.789 --> 00:48:44.922
… Gen: to people, whether the people are
stubborn or the people are inclusive.

00:48:45.603 --> 00:48:46.223
Chris: All right, awesome.

00:48:46.507 --> 00:48:48.550
Uh, that wraps us up
with exactly one second.

00:48:48.553 --> 00:48:49.959
Thank you, Gen. You've been amazing.

00:48:50.015 --> 00:48:52.479
Uh, and thank you everybody for
attending this session with Gen Herres.

00:48:52.500 --> 00:48:55.586
You can continue the conversation
